import json
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).parent;r=json.loads((p/'reviews.json').read_text());urls=json.loads((p/'geo2025-urls.json').read_text())
rows={
'10':[(4.5,'Number theory','Modulo four forces evenness, and iterating the argument after division gives infinite descent.',r'Find all integer triples $(x,y,z)$ satisfying $x^2(1-y^2)+y^2+z^2=0$.'),(3,'Algebra','Compare the increasing function t to the power 2n minus t at the two numbers, using a single square inequality.',None),(5,'Geometry','Similarities convert the reciprocal-distance sum into one reciprocal altitude; its extremum occurs at a perpendicular line.',r'A ray from the origin makes an acute angle $\alpha$ with the positive $x$-axis. Fix $M=(a,b)$ strictly inside this angle. A line through $M$ meets the ray at $L$ and the positive $x$-axis at $K$. Find the largest possible value of $1/ML+1/MK$.'),(5.5,'Geometry','Locate both circumcentres on chord bisectors, then express their distances with complementary central angles to obtain a common homothety ratio.',r'On a semicircle with centre $O$ and diameter $AB$, points occur in the order $A,C,D,B$. Let $M,P,N$ be the midpoints of $AC,CD,DB$. The circumcentres of $ACP$ and $BDP$ are $O_1,O_2$. Prove $MN\parallel O_1O_2$.'),(5,'Combinatorics','Overlapping triangles force period two under a hypothetical three-colouring; blocks of five give an explicit four-colouring.',r'A function $f:\mathbb Z_{\ge0}\to\mathbb Z_{\ge0}$ satisfies $f(x)\ne f(y)$ whenever $|x-y|\in\{5,7,12\}$. What is the smallest possible size of its image?')],
'11-12':[(2.5,'Algebra','Substitute zero to remove the triple composition, then a cancelling absolute-value input determines every requested value.',None),(3.5,'Number theory','Two linear combinations force the gcd to divide 2025 squared minus one; a coprime pair attains that divisor.',None),(4,'Number theory','Split into a high binary block and the bitwise complement of that block; the numbers of one-bits add to 2025.',r'Can $2025\cdot2^{2025}-2025$ be expressed as a sum of exactly 2025 distinct nonnegative integral powers of 2?'),(5.5,'Geometry','Two altitude identities give reciprocal similarities; an auxiliary perpendicular foot creates two cyclic quadrilaterals for the final angle chase.',r'Convex $ABCD$ has $AB>AD$ and $\angle ABC=\angle ADC=90^\circ$. Choose $M\in AB$ with $AM=AD$, and put $K=MD\cap BC$. The line through $K$ perpendicular to $AC$ meets $AB$ at $X$. Let $N$ be the foot from $D$ to $AC$. Prove $\angle MNK=\angle MCX$.'),(6,'Geometry','Recognize two cyclic quadrilaterals, then use directed power products on EF; midpoint coordinates transfer the third cyclicity.',r'In acute $ABC$, take $D\in AC$, $K\in BC$ with $DK\parallel AB$, then $E\in AD$, $F\in CK$. Put $M=EF\cap DK$ and $N=EF\cap AB$. Suppose $\angle CAF=\angle CBE$ and the midpoint $P$ of $EF$ lies on $(ABM)$, with $P\ne M$. Prove $P\in(KDN)$.')]}
for g,rr in rows.items():
 for n,(v,t,why,st) in enumerate(rr,1):
  slug=f'geo_2025_g{g}_{n}';src=urls[g];su=src+f'#page={n}';sol='[Official worked solution (Georgian)]('+su+').';kind='source-link';notes=''
  if g=='10' and n==1:
   sol=r'Rewrite the equation as $x^2+y^2+z^2=x^2y^2$. Squares modulo 4 show first that $x,y$ cannot both be odd, then that precisely one cannot be odd either; hence all three variables are even. After writing $x=2u,y=2v,z=2w$, obtain $u^2+v^2+w^2=4u^2v^2$. A sum of three squares divisible by 4 has all three terms even. Repeating shows the original variables are divisible by arbitrarily high powers of 2. Thus the only solution is $(0,0,0)$.';kind='original-exposition'
  if g=='10' and n==5:
   sol=r'The answer is **4**. The triples $(x,x+5,x+12)$ and $(x,x+7,x+12)$ must each receive three different values. If only three values were available, their shared endpoints would force $f(x+5)=f(x+7)$ for every $x\ge0$. Thus $f(t)=f(t+2)$ for $t\ge5$, in particular $f(5)=f(17)$, contradicting their difference 12. For a four-value construction, take $f(n)=\lfloor n/5\rfloor\pmod4$. Increasing $n$ by 5 changes the quotient by 1; increasing it by 7 changes it by 1 or 2; increasing it by 12 changes it by 2 or 3. None is zero modulo 4.';kind='original-exposition'
  if g=='11-12' and n==3:
   sol=r'Yes. More generally, for $N\ge1$ write $$N2^N-N=(N-1)2^N+\bigl(2^N-1-(N-1)\bigr).$$ The first summand is the binary expansion of $N-1$ shifted $N$ places. The second occupies only the bottom $N$ places, and is the complement there of the binary digits of $N-1$. If $N-1$ has $k$ one-bits, the two disjoint blocks therefore contain $k$ and $N-k$ one-bits. Their sum has exactly $N$ one-bits. Set $N=2025$.';kind='original-exposition'
  if g=='11-12' and n==5:
   sol=r'The angle hypothesis makes $A,E,F,B$ concyclic. Since $DK\parallel AB$, an angle chase also makes $D,K,E,F$ concyclic. Give the line $EF$ a directed coordinate with $P=0$, $E=-h$, $F=h$, $M=t$, and $N=s$. Taking the power of $N$ in the circles $(AEFB)$ and $(ABMP)$ gives $$s^2-h^2=(s-t)s,$$ so $st=h^2$. The power of $M$ in $(DKEF)$ is $t^2-h^2$. Its directed product toward $N,P$ is $t(t-s)=t^2-st=t^2-h^2$. The converse of the intersecting-secants identity therefore puts $D,K,N,P$ on one circle, as required.';kind='original-exposition'
  (p/(slug+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps({'country':'Georgia','competition':'Georgian National Mathematical Olympiad','year':2025,'section':'Third Round — Grade '+g,'problem_number':str(n),'problem_markdown':st or 'See the official paper for the full statement.','solutions_markdown':[]},ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
  r[slug]={'difficulty':v,'topic':t,'tags':[],'reason':why,'number':str(n),'sourceUrl':src,'solutionUrl':su,'solution':sol,'solutionKind':kind,'sourceName':'NAEC — official national final paper and solutions','license':'Original competition rights retained; concise English mathematical paraphrase.','notes':notes}
(p/'reviews.json').write_text(json.dumps(r,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
